<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">28 August 1963 · Lincoln Memorial, Washington, D.C.</p><h1 class="sp-title">Martin Luther King Jr.: I Have a Dream</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>At the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, before a crowd of some 250,000 people, Martin Luther King Jr. set aside part of his prepared text to describe his vision of a nation free of racial injustice.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>...not be judged by the color of their skin but by the content of their character.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Speech at the Lincoln Memorial, 28 August 1963. Quoted briefly: the full text is still under copyright.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://www.npr.org/2010/01/18/122701268/i-have-a-dream-speech-in-its-entirety" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">NPR</span><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“...not be judged by the color of their skin but by the content of their character.” — Martin Luther King Jr., Martin Luther King Jr.: I Have a Dream" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-mlk-i-have-a-dream.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The march helped build support for the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, which outlawed segregation and protected the right of Black Americans to vote. King was assassinated in Memphis in 1968.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a></div></div></section>
